# Texts of the MCP server. Tool descriptions are shown to code agents and stay in English in every language.

mcp-server-instructions = CodeSkimr is the reference for the code you write. Search for existing symbols before adding new ones, and read the architecture before deciding where code belongs.
mcp-tool-search-symbols = Find existing symbols (types, functions, constants) by name. Use it before writing new code to check whether something similar already exists. Returns ranked hits with kind and file.
mcp-tool-get-symbol = Show one symbol: signature, documentation, members, metrics and the architecture layer it belongs to. Accepts a symbol id or a qualified name.
mcp-tool-get-architecture = Show the architecture of a project: its layers with their namespaces and the dependencies between the layers, including violations and cycles.
mcp-tool-find-usages = Find where a symbol is used (direction incoming, the default) or what it uses (outgoing), up to a depth. Each result has the kind of use, the location and the confidence; a probable result is the resolver's best guess, so check it. A result with verified false was found by name resolution only and is not checked by a compiler or indexer; verified results are listed first, and the answer counts both apart (preliminary is true while any result is unchecked). References from tests are listed only with include_tests.
mcp-tool-impact-of-change = Show what a change of a symbol can reach: the affected symbols with their distance, layer and whether they are public interfaces. Symbols that only verified references lead to come first and are counted apart from the others; preliminary is true while any listed symbol is unchecked. Use it before changing a signature or behaviour.
mcp-tool-find-similar-code = Check whether code like this already exists: pass a function (symbol) or a snippet with its language, and get the stored functions with a similar syntax structure, most similar first, with location and similarity in percent. Identifiers and literals are ignored, so renamed copies are found. Use it before writing a new function; the answer is as old as the snapshot it names.
mcp-tool-find-constants = Find existing constants by name before declaring a new one. Each result has the location and the declaration as written, which usually shows the value. The answer also lists configuration keys (TOML files) and translation message ids (Fluent files) of the project folder whose name contains the query, when the caller may read source; `keys_searched` says whether that was done.
mcp-tool-check-conventions = Check files against the code rules (function length, duplicated functions) and get violations with error codes. Pass the paths of your change; without paths the whole project is checked. The files are read as they are now, not as the last scan saw them. Rules the check cannot verify yet are listed in not_checked, so a clean answer does not mean all rules are met.
mcp-tool-get-project = Show a registered project with its languages and the tools that exact usages (level 2) need for each language. The check runs now: for every tool you get its state (ready, missing, too_old or version_unknown), what it is needed for, how to install it and the installed and the required version, and each language says level2_ready. Call it before you rely on usages of a language: without level 2 they are unchecked. A missing tool is an answer, not an error.
mcp-tool-get-dependencies = Show what a symbol, namespace, type or layer depends on (direction outgoing, the default) or what depends on it (incoming). Each dependency has its layer, the number of references and whether it breaks the layering the project declares; layers sums them up per layer. Use it to check that a change keeps the architecture, and pass layer instead of symbol to see how one layer relates to the others.
mcp-tool-rescan = Scan the project again so that the other tools see your latest changes. Only files that changed since the last scan are processed. Returns the new snapshot with the number of files, symbols and references and how long the scan took. It is the only tool that changes anything, and it needs the scan permission.
